1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,300
Divide Tesla's Q3 operating profit by the cars it sold, and you get about $1,500 per car.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:09,600
That's under 2 million won, roughly the price of a new smartphone.

3
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:16,660
In its best quarter, Q1 2022, it was $11,600 per car. Now it's one-eighth of that.

4
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:22,820
Strange, isn't it? Tesla's market value is $1.5 trillion.

5
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:27,350
Why does the market pay so much for a carmaker that keeps a smartphone's worth per car?

6
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:31,760
Watch to the end and you'll learn three things.

7
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:38,710
One: the 'free money' that propped up Tesla's profits for years. Two: where the missing profit actually went.

8
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:52,020
Three: by Benjamin Graham's method, Buffett's teacher, how much Tesla must earn per share to justify today's price. Plus the companies Buffett admits he missed because of such strict rules.

9
00:00:52,830 --> 00:00:55,500
First, where does Tesla's money come from?

10
00:00:56,320 --> 00:01:05,170
Of $28 billion in Q3 revenue, nearly three-quarters came from cars. The rest: services like charging and insurance, and batteries for homes and power plants.

11
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:11,420
And at the bottom, about $100 million in regulatory credits. Looks tiny, right? It's the first key to today's story.

12
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:17,570
Tesla sold its cars for about $41,000 each on average, almost the same as a year ago.

13
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:22,010
The funny thing is, the business of building and selling cars actually got better.

14
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:29,180
What's left after production costs rose from 15.4% of sales a year ago to 18.1%.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,070
Yet total operating profit fell 56% in a year, from $1.62 billion to $718 million.

16
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:42,830
Better car business, half the profit. There are two culprits. Let's meet the first.

17
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:46,260
Promise number one: the free money.

18
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:55,390
Governments set emissions rules for carmakers and fine those who miss them. Companies that beat the rules earn credits.

19
00:01:55,610 --> 00:02:07,460
Selling only EVs, Tesla has credits to spare, so it sells them to other carmakers. They cost almost nothing to make, so nearly all of it is profit.

20
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:15,290
That income fell 73%, from $417 million a year ago to $113 million.

21
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:21,300
The free money shrank to a quarter in a year. A pay cut like that would have anyone polishing their resume.

22
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:29,110
How important was it? In 2020, Tesla's first profitable year, net income was $721 million.

23
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:36,150
But credit sales that year were $1.58 billion. By simple math, without them Tesla might have posted a loss.

24
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:46,070
Why? One of the biggest reasons is U.S. law. Last July, the fine for missing fuel-economy standards was cut to zero.

25
00:02:46,290 --> 00:02:55,470
No fine means less rush to sell EVs and less reason to buy credits. Tesla's free-money faucet started closing.

26
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:00,990
The second culprit is a surprise. Tesla invited it in itself.

27
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:05,470
Promise number two: where did the profit go?

28
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:13,280
Operating profit fell $900 million, while R&D rose $1.1 billion, more than the drop.

29
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:19,670
R&D rose 69% in a year, from $1.63 billion to $2.76 billion.

30
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:29,270
Capex on factories and equipment also more than doubled, from $2.25 billion to $4.73 billion. That hits cash in hand more than profit.

31
00:03:30,090 --> 00:03:37,570
Per car: Tesla spent nearly $5,700 on R&D and still kept about $1,500.

32
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:42,830
Had R&D stayed at last year's level, it would have kept roughly $3,800 per car, by simple math.

33
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:50,420
Like a family whose salary is flat but doubled the kids' tuition. The bank balance looks worrying, but what matters is where it goes.

34
00:03:51,240 --> 00:04:03,140
With car sales down from a year ago, Tesla is betting on businesses that earn after the sale: AI computers that train self-driving, the Cybercab robotaxi, and the Optimus robot.

35
00:04:03,970 --> 00:04:10,390
Capex through September alone was $13 billion, already past last year's full $8.5 billion with a quarter to go.

36
00:04:10,610 --> 00:04:14,280
And Tesla says this spending will keep growing for the next few years.

37
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:23,660
In Q3, over $6.2 billion of cash came in from operations, but only $1.5 billion was left after investment, less than half of last year's $4 billion.

38
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:29,530
Luckily Tesla holds $45.4 billion in cash and investments, so it isn't short of money.

39
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,850
So what does the market expect this spending to produce?

40
00:04:35,670 --> 00:04:39,250
Three reasons some people don't see Tesla as just a carmaker.

41
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:49,850
One: FSD, the driver-assist software. Paying users, by purchase or subscription, rose 67% in a year, from 1.04 million to 1.74 million.

42
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:57,930
Tesla says more than three in four new buyers in North America pay for it. Money keeps coming after the car is sold.

43
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:06,560
Two: driverless robotaxis. They run in several U.S. cities, and in September the steering-wheel-free Cybercab began carrying riders in Austin.

44
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:16,610
Three: the Optimus humanoid robot. Production has begun in Fremont, but first deliveries to outside companies are expected around late 2027. The hands are still a problem.

45
00:05:17,430 --> 00:05:24,690
Some change already shows up in numbers. Gross profit from services like charging, insurance and used cars doubled in a year.

46
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:34,600
Battery revenue fell 16%, yet installations rose 10%. And it keeps $33 of every $100 sold, nearly double cars.

47
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:41,970
So how big must this future be to justify today's price? Let's borrow the calculator of Buffett's teacher, Graham.

48
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:44,700
Promise number three.

49
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:47,460
Warren Buffett always asks two questions.

50
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:52,230
First, is it a business with steady, predictable profits and little debt?

51
00:05:52,450 --> 00:06:01,240
Second, is the price right? Here his teacher Benjamin Graham was strict: don't look at the best year, use average profits over many years.

52
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:09,450
Over the past year Tesla earned about $1.08 per share. Averaged over several years, about about $0.97.

53
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:19,710
A company earning $1 a year on average is valued at about $10, a conservative math with no growth. That gives a ValueNote fair price of $9.72 per share.

54
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:26,870
At $372.2 (Oct 23 close), the stock trades at 38.3x that fair price.

55
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:36,570
Yes, I thought my calculator was broken too. But that's the nature of this method: it counts today's earnings and pays nothing for dreams.

56
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:44,870
Flip it around: for today's price to fit the same math, Tesla must earn about $37 per share on average.

57
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:54,670
Profit must grow to about 34x today's level (about $1.08 per share). In other words, the market is betting Tesla becomes a company earning dozens of times more.

58
00:06:55,500 --> 00:07:02,310
ValueNote runs this same calculation, based on Buffett, Charlie Munger and Graham, for over 600 companies.

59
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:13,920
On the same yardstick, Tesla's ValueNote score is 26: 51 for financial strength, but 0 for price, meaning the price is very high relative to current profit.

60
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:18,610
It only looks at today's earnings, so don't read it as a buy or sell signal.

61
00:07:19,430 --> 00:07:25,070
But this strict calculator once made Warren Buffett himself miss big. Twice.

62
00:07:25,890 --> 00:07:29,950
Buffett himself admitted that not investing in Amazon and Google was a mistake.

63
00:07:30,170 --> 00:07:37,040
Companies that pour earnings into the future always look expensive on today's profits. Whether Tesla is one of them, nobody knows yet.

64
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:45,100
If robotaxis and subscriptions become big money, today's spending is seed, not waste, and the average-profit math is too low.

65
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:55,870
If commercialization slips, Tesla waits with the free money gone and costs up, as with the delayed Optimus deliveries.

66
00:07:56,090 --> 00:08:02,360
So this isn't a buy or sell call. It's about seeing what future is priced in.

67
00:08:03,180 --> 00:08:08,020
Finally, three numbers that will show which way this story goes at the next earnings.

68
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:16,070
One: car margin excluding credits, now 18.1%, the strength of the car business without free money.

69
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:24,410
Two: cash left after investment. Below zero means dipping into savings, and in Q2 it actually was negative.

70
00:08:24,630 --> 00:08:30,510
Three: paid FSD users and robotaxi cities, the speed at which dreams turn into revenue.

71
00:08:30,730 --> 00:08:42,110
Try it on your own stocks: in the earnings tables, find non-core lines like credits, subsidies or one-offs and subtract them from operating profit. For Tesla, $700 million becomes $600 million.

72
00:08:42,930 --> 00:08:50,510
To sum up: Tesla keeps about $1,500 per car. The car business improved, but the free money from credits is drying up.

73
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:57,220
The missing profit didn't vanish: it's going into R&D for AI, robotaxis and robots, and spare cash into capex.

74
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:03,710
And today's price assumes that spending turns Tesla into a company earning dozens of times more.

75
